From dfab8ca8ceddb16dce1fc609ef4c693aed18d8e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:01:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban pickup-stall: no hold repair once the live
 hold lifted; close host-wide repairs with nothing left

Problem: on 11-13 at 15:45:09Z worker-6 filed t_01a1d59589cb8efc "Pickup held on worker-6: oldest
t_01a1d58ace44b467 waiting 11 min". The registry entry quoted in its own body already read state='active',
hold=False, hold_owner=None; the card's last `hold` skip was 15:43:45Z. That card ran at 15:55Z, and the alarm
sat `ready` until it was archived by hand at 16:18Z. Same shape on 11-12 07:18Z (t_01a1d3c595358fa2, closed
"No move needed").

Why 1: file_repairs groups a stalled card by its newest recorded skip reason (_group -> `hold`), a historical
  record. Evidence: alarm body "last skip hold (held), first 15:34:36Z, last 15:43:45Z, 8 pass(es)".
Why 2: the hold had ended before the filing pass. Evidence: same body, registry at 15:45:09Z hold=False.
  (hypothesis, timing only: worker-6's core-rollout hold around its 15:39Z gateway restart.)
Why 3: the only live-registry gate on the hold group was _hold_expected -> expected_hold(), which returns None
  for `hold is not True` (kanban_pickup_stall.py expected_hold), and None means "alert as usual". A lifted
  hold took the same path as a hold with no stated release.
Root (happened): the host-wide `hold` repair was filed from skip records without requiring the hold to still
  be present in the live registry entry the pass reads.
Root (not caught): no test covered "hold lifted between the last hold skip and the filing pass"; the existing
  burst test even fed `hold` skips against an unheld registry. The 11-12 false alarm was closed "No move
  needed" with no route back to the producer.
Second gap: nothing closed an open host-wide repair. Searched hermes_cli for pickup-stall-detector /
  KEY_PREFIX / archive/complete callers: none; sweep() only filed (and only when cards were stalled).
Recurrence: t_01a1cec7cd7fe7ce (rollout hold -> expected_hold), t_01a1d3c595358fa2, t_01a1d59589cb8efc.

Countermeasures:
- symptom/root: file_repairs skips the `hold` group when the live registry entry is readable and not held
  (_hold_lifted). An unreadable registry still alerts. The card's next pickup skip classifies it on a later
  pass (e.g. profile_ineligible gets its own card, budget_exhausted the queue card).
- second gap: new kanban_pickup_stall_resolve.sweep runs every detector pass, before filing. It archives an
  open, unclaimed host-wide repair about this host when every card it names has left ready here (done /
  archived / blocked on the Board, gone, re-pinned, leased, or imported), or when it is a `hold` repair and
  the live hold lifted. The closing comment names each card's state. A pickup_stall_resolved event marks the
  close, so _covering_repair opens a fresh card for a later stall instead of reusing it for an hour.
  Claimed, running, blocked, in-review or Board-leased repairs are left to their run.
- detection: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_pickup_stall_lifted.py (RED on 93e9a44e67: 3 of 5 fail, namely
  lifted-hold filing, close-when-left-ready, close-when-hold-lifted; GREEN after). A real operator hold
  (hold=True, no stated release, not the rollout's) still files exactly one card per host
  (test_a_real_operator_hold_still_files_exactly_one_card_per_host and the existing
  test_an_operator_hold_with_no_stated_release_still_files_a_card / test_a_held_machine_files_one_hold_card).
  test_same_cause_burst_...[hold] now holds the registry, as a real hold burst does.

Card: t_01a1d5b42df173d3
